{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"unordered-containers","title":"unordered_map & unordered_set","group":"STL Containers & Iterators","order":53,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Hash tables: average O(1) insert, erase and lookup, no ordering. The workhorse for frequency counts, “seen before?” checks and caches.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Usage</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_map&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_set&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; nums{2, 7, 11, 15};\n    int target = 18;\n    std::unordered_map&lt;int, int&gt; seen;              // value → index\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; static_cast&lt;int&gt;(nums.size()); ++i) {\n        if (auto it = seen.find(target - nums[i]); it != seen.end())\n            std::cout &lt;&lt; \"two-sum: \" &lt;&lt; it-&gt;second &lt;&lt; ',' &lt;&lt; i &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n        seen[nums[i]] = i;\n    }\n\n    std::unordered_set&lt;std::string&gt; visited;\n    for (std::string city : {\"paris\", \"rome\", \"paris\"})\n        if (!visited.insert(city).second) std::cout &lt;&lt; \"repeat: \" &lt;&lt; city &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; visited.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; visited.contains(\"rome\") &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>two-sum: 1,2\nrepeat: paris\n2 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Iteration order of unordered containers is unspecified and can change after a rehash or between library versions. Never print or depend on it — copy into a <code>std::vector</code> and sort if you need a stable order.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>Buckets, load factor, rehashing</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_map&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::unordered_map&lt;int, int&gt; m;\n    m.reserve(1000);                              // pre-size buckets: avoids rehashing while filling\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; ++i) m[i] = i * i;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; m.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (m.bucket_count() &gt;= 1000) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (m.load_factor() &lt;= m.max_load_factor()) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; m.max_load_factor() &lt;&lt; '\\n';     // default 1.0: rehash when size &gt; buckets\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1000 1 1\n1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-fingerprint\"></i>Hashing your own type</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;cstddef&gt;\n#include &lt;functional&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_set&gt;\n\nstruct Point {\n    int x, y;\n    bool operator==(const Point&amp;) const = default;          // needed for collisions\n};\n\nstruct PointHash {\n    std::size_t operator()(const Point&amp; p) const noexcept {\n        std::size_t h1 = std::hash&lt;int&gt;{}(p.x), h2 = std::hash&lt;int&gt;{}(p.y);\n        return h1 ^ (h2 + 0x9e3779b9 + (h1 &lt;&lt; 6) + (h1 &gt;&gt; 2));   // hash_combine\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    std::unordered_set&lt;Point, PointHash&gt; cells{{0, 0}, {1, 2}, {0, 0}};\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; cells.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; cells.count({1, 2}) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>unordered_map</code></th><th><code>map</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Hash table (buckets of nodes)</td><td>Red-black tree</td></tr><tr><td>Lookup</td><td>O(1) avg, O(n) worst</td><td>O(log n) always</td></tr><tr><td>Order</td><td>None</td><td>Sorted by key</td></tr><tr><td>Key needs</td><td><code>std::hash&lt;K&gt;</code> + <code>==</code></td><td><code>&lt;</code></td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>Higher (buckets)</td><td>3 pointers + colour per node</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>In competitive programming, adversarial inputs can force many collisions in <code>std::unordered_map&lt;int, …&gt;</code> (the default <code>std::hash&lt;int&gt;</code> is the identity), turning O(1) into O(n). Use a randomised custom hash, or <code>std::map</code>.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How does an unordered_map handle collisions?</summary><div><p>The standard containers use <em>separate chaining</em>: each bucket holds a linked list of the nodes whose hash maps there. When the load factor (size / bucket count) exceeds <code>max_load_factor</code> (default 1.0), the table allocates more buckets and rehashes every element.</p></div></details></section>"}